    figure table aspect 3:2 members ["h_person","h_d","h_y1","h_y0","h_t","rule_h","r1_name","r1_d","r1_y1","r1_y0","r1_t","r1_q","r1_qt","r2_name","r2_d","r2_y1","r2_y0","r2_t","r2_q","r2_qt","r3_name","r3_d","r3_y1","r3_y0","r3_t","r3_q","r3_qt","r4_name","r4_d","r4_y1","r4_y0","r4_t","r4_q","r4_qt","rule_a","avg_lab","avg_y1","avg_y0","avg_t","sv_lab","sv_y1","sv_y0","sv_t"] hidden true

    card title "The column that is always blank"

So let's pretend, just this once, that we could see both futures, for four people.
    draw h_person h_y1 h_y0 h_t rule_h

Maria's row holds her twenty-four and her nineteen, and the ga (@draw r1_t@)p between them, five.
    draw r1_name r1_y1 r1_y0

James, Rosa and Daniel get two numbers each. Rosa is the sickes (@highlight r3_y0 effect circle@)t of the four, and James and Daniel the healthiest.
    draw r2_name r2_y1 r2_y0 r3_name r3_y1 r3_y0 r4_name r4_y1 r4_y0

Their own effects run down the last column: four, three and four good days. Those who will sign up  (@highlight r1_t r3_t effect circle@)gain four on average, the same as everyone.
    draw r2_t r3_t r4_t

Averaged over the four, coverage adds four good days a month.  (@highlight avg_t effect circle@)Economists call that the average treatment effect.
    draw rule_a avg_lab avg_y1 avg_y0 avg_t

But in the real world, nobody shows us both columns: each of them chose whether to sign up.
    draw h_d r1_d r2_d r3_d r4_d

The two in worse health, Maria and Rosa, signed up. James and Daniel didn't bother.
    highlight r1_d r3_d effect box

So for Maria and Rosa we see only t (@draw r1_q r2_q r3_q r4_q parallel true@)he coverage column, and for James and Daniel only the other one.
    hide r1_y0 r2_y1 r3_y0 r4_y1

And the effect column, (@draw r1_qt r2_qt r3_qt r4_qt parallel true@) the one we came for, goes blank all the way down.
    hide r1_t r2_t r3_t r4_t

Half the table is missing, and which half depends on what each person cho (@highlight r1_q r2_q r3_q r4_q@)se. That is why a causal question is a missing-data problem.
    fade rule_a avg_lab avg_y1 avg_y0 avg_t to 0.25

A survey fills the gap the obvious way: it compares the insured with the uninsured.
    draw sv_lab

    quiz "The two sicker people signed up. Will the survey's gap, insured minus uninsured, come out above the true four days, below it, or about the same?"
        intro: Before the sum, a prediction.
        right: Below: the people who signed up started out sicker, so their numbers sit low whatever coverage did for them.
        wrong: Look at who signed up: the healthier two, or the sicker two?
        * Above four
        + Below four
        * About four

The insured, Maria and Rosa, average twenty-one good day (@draw sv_y0@)s. The uninsured average twenty-five.
    draw sv_y1

So the survey says coverage costs four good days, when on average it adds four.
    draw sv_t

That gap of eight is  (@highlight avg_t sv_t effect circle@)not insurance at all. It is who chose it: the insured started out eight days sicker.
    fade avg_t to 1

To borrow honestly, we'd need uninsured people who differ from the insured in nothing but the coverage.
    highlight r1_q r3_q

## A lottery lends a stand-in
voice: female
level: basic
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein, Taubman, Wright, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Allen, Baicker and the Oregon Health Study Group
      year: 2012
      finding: Oregon's 2008 lottery drew names from a Medicaid waiting list; in the first year, winners were about 25 percentage points more likely to be covered, used more care, had less financial strain and reported better health.
    - id: baicker2013
      title: 'The Oregon Experiment: Effects of Medicaid on Clinical Outcomes'
      authors: Baicker, Taubman, Allen, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Schneider, Wright, Zaslavsky, Finkelstein and the Oregon Health Study Group
      year: 2013
      finding: About 30,000 names were drawn from a waiting list of almost 90,000; 12,229 people interviewed and examined about two years later.

    shape list_box thick #3d3833 shape rect x 145 y 430 width 270 height 180 radius 12 hidden true
    text list_txt "90,000 names" cites ["baicker2013"] x 145 y 460 font_size 50 hidden true
    text list_sub "10,000 places" #8f887c cites ["baicker2013"] x 145 y 395 font_size 42 hidden true
    arrow arr_d #3d3833 from.x 285 from.y 470 to.x 360 to.y 560 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    arrow arr_n dashed #3d3833 from.x 285 from.y 390 to.x 360 to.y 300 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text lab_d "Drawn" x 470 y 580 font_size 62 hidden true
    text lab_d_n "~30,000" #8f887c cites ["baicker2013"] x 470 y 515 font_size 42 hidden true
    text lab_n "Not drawn" x 470 y 320 font_size 62 hidden true
    text lab_n_n "~60,000" #8f887c x 470 y 255 font_size 42 hidden true
    icon crowd_d size 90 x 650 y 560 or ["people"] icon_look picture of "busts in silhouette" hidden true
    icon crowd_n size 90 x 650 y 300 or ["people"] icon_look picture of "busts in silhouette" hidden true
    math h_y1 tex Y_i(1) size 56 colors {"Y_i(1)":"#2f6b8f"} x 830 y 690 hidden true
    math h_y0 tex Y_i(0) size 56 colors {"Y_i(0)":"#b5482e"} x 1020 y 690 hidden true
    path rule_h thick #3d3833 points [[740,645],[1110,645]] hidden true
    text d_y1 "25" #2f6b8f x 830 y 560 font_size 76 hidden true
    text d_y0 "21" #b5482e x 1020 y 560 font_size 76 hidden true
    text n_y1 "25" #2f6b8f x 830 y 300 font_size 76 hidden true
    text n_y0 "21" #b5482e x 1020 y 300 font_size 76 hidden true
    text d_q "?" #8f887c x 1020 y 560 font_size 86 hidden true
    text n_q "?" #8f887c x 830 y 300 font_size 86 hidden true
    arrow stand curved #b5482e from.x 1085 from.y 330 to.x 1085 to.y 530 style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    math eq tex "E[\\,Y_i \\mid Z_i = 1\\,] - E[\\,Y_i \\mid Z_i = 0\\,] = 25 - 21 = 4" size 52 colors {"4":"#8a5fa8","21":"#b5482e","25":"#2f6b8f"} x 590 y 160 hidden true
    text enrol "3 in 10 enrol" #8a5fa8 cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 470 y 450 font_size 42 hidden true
    text offer "offer: 1.2" #8a5fa8 x 330 y 55 font_size 52 hidden true
    math eq2 tex "\\frac{1.2}{0.3} = 4" size 52 colors {"4":"#8a5fa8"} x 700 y 55 hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["list_box","list_txt","list_sub","arr_d","arr_n","lab_d","lab_d_n","lab_n","lab_n_n","crowd_d","crowd_n","h_y1","h_y0","rule_h","d_y1","d_y0","n_y1","n_y0","d_q","n_q","stand","eq","enrol","offer","eq2"] hidden true

    card title "A lottery lends a stand-in"

So where would we find uninsured people just like the insured? In 2008, Oregon made some.
    draw list_box list_txt

The state had money to add a (@draw list_sub@)bout ten thousand adults to Medicaid, and about ninety th (@highlight list_txt@)ousand put their names down.

So it drew names by lottery: about thirty thousand were drawn and invited to apply. Call being drawn Z equals one.
    draw arr_d lab_d lab_d_n arr_n lab_n lab_n_n

A lottery knows nothing about anyone's health, so on average each side gets the same mix of Marias and Daniels.
    highlight lab_d lab_n

Picture our four peopl (@draw h_y1 h_y0 rule_h@)e copied thousands of times, and split by the draw.
    draw crowd_d crowd_n parallel true

On average, both sides would score twenty-five good days with coverage and twenty-one without: the averages from our table.
    draw d_y1 d_y0 n_y1 n_y0

Keep it a toy for now: everyone drawn enrols, and nobody else gets  (@draw d_q n_q parallel true@)coverage. So each side lives only one future.
    hide d_y0 n_y1

The drawn side's life without coverage is a blank, just like Maria's.
    highlight d_q effect circle

But because chance alone chose the sides, the other side's twenty-one can stand in for it.
    draw stand

Twenty-five minus twenty-one: four good days, the true average effect, though nobody's own effect was ever seen.
    draw eq

The survey, with the same kind of people, said minus four. The only thing that changed is who decided: a person, or a draw.
    highlight eq part "4" effect circle

Now the real wrinkle: in Oregon, only about three in t (@highlight lab_d@)en of those drawn ended up enrolled.
    draw enrol

In our toy, that pulls the drawn side down to about twenty-two, and the plai (@draw offer@)n gap shrinks to about one point two: the effect of the offer.
    fade d_y1 d_q n_q n_y0 stand to 0.3

So the gap from the offer, divided by the rise in enrolment the draw c (@highlight enrol@)aused, gives back four: the effect of c (@highlight eq2 part "4" effect circle@)overage for the people the draw moved onto it.
    draw eq2

## More data shrinks chance, not choice
voice: female
level: basic
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation:
      - y = 10*sqrt(2/x)
      - y = 8 + 0*x
    params:
      'n': {value: 2, min: 2, max: 10000, label: people per side}
    x_range:
      - 2
      - 10000
    x_scale: log
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 12
    show_equation: false
    x_label: people on each side
    y_label: typical miss (good days)
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: 'n', curve: 0, label: false, guides: false, id: you}
adjust:
    x_label:
      scale: 1.5
      move: [-4, 0]
    y_label:
      scale: 1.5
      move: [4, 0]

    text readout "6,000 a side: 0.18 days" #2f6b8f x 600 y 230 font_size 38 hidden true
    label lab_survey "Survey: who chose" above #b5482e attach_to curve_1 font_size 40 hidden true
    label lab_lottery "Lottery: chance" right #2f6b8f attach_to curve_0 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "More data shrinks chance, not choice"

But a draw can be unlucky too: with only four people, the two sides could easily differ by chance.
    draw axes

So how far off is each method as t (@highlight x_label y_label@)he groups grow? Across, people on  (@draw curve_1@)each side; up, the typical miss in good days.

The survey is off by eight good days, because the sicker pe (@highlight curve_1@)ople chose coverage, and asking more people changes nothing about that.
    draw lab_survey

A lottery is off only by chance. If good days vary by about ten from person to person (@draw you@), two people a side can miss by ten.
    draw curve_0 lab_lottery

Move the point to the right, to bigger groups, and watch how far off each method stays.
    explore params ["n"] watch "Watch the point slide to bigger groups, and see how far off each method stays."

The lottery's miss falls toward zero, while the survey's stays at eight.
    animate params.n.value 6000 duration 4

It shrinks with the square root of the group: four times the people, half the miss.
    highlight curve_0

With six thousand a side, about the size of Oregon's health (@point at.ref you gesture circle@) survey, chance is worth less than a quarter of a good day.
    draw readout

So the survey's problem is who chose, not how many it asked.
    highlight curve_1

More data shrinks the noise of chance. It cannot undo a choice.
    highlight curve_0 curve_1

Oregon's real margins were wider than this toy's, partly because most winners never enrolled. But they were margins of chance, and they shrink with numbers.
    highlight curve_0

So a big lottery leaves only a small, honest margin of error, and that is the kind of number Oregon could report.
    highlight lab_lottery

## What Oregon found
voice: female
level: basic
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein et al. (Oregon Health Study Group)
      year: 2012
      finding: In the first year, lottery winners used more health care, had less financial strain and medical debt, and reported better physical and mental health.
    - id: baicker2013
      title: 'The Oregon Experiment: Effects of Medicaid on Clinical Outcomes'
      authors: Baicker et al. (Oregon Health Study Group), New England Journal of Medicine
      year: 2013
      finding: 'About 25 months after the lottery, 12,229 people in the Portland area: Medicaid coverage lowered positive depression screens by 9.15 percentage points (95% CI -16.70 to -1.60; control mean 30.0%) and catastrophic out-of-pocket spending by 4.48 points (-8.26 to -0.69); no significant effect on elevated blood pressure (-1.33; -7.16 to 4.49) or glycated haemoglobin of 6.5% or more (-0.93; -4.44 to 2.59; control mean 5.1%); diabetes diagnosis rose 3.83 points (1.93 to 5.73) and diabetes medication use 5.43 points (1.39 to 9.48).'
takeaways:
    - An effect compares two futures
    - One future is always missing
    - A lottery lends a fair stand-in

