    card title "What Oregon found"

So what did Oregon's draw show about coverage itself?
    draw year1_box year1_line_1

In the first year, those drawn (@draw year1_line_2@) used more care, had les (@draw year1_line_3@)s medical debt, and said  (@draw year1_line_4@)they  (@draw year1_line_5@)felt healthier.

    erase year1

About two years in, researchers interviewed and examined some twelve thousand people in and around Portland.
    draw axis tick_m15 tick_m10 tick_m5 tick_0 tick_5 axis_lab null_line cite_b

Each row is the effect of coverage itself for the people the draw moved onto it: the offer's gap divided by the rise in enrolment. The dashed line is no effect.
    highlight null_line

Depression: about nine points lower, from a base of thirty percent, and its range of plausible values stays clear of zero.
    draw study_0 ci_0 study_label_0 est_label_0

Catastrophic medical bills: about four and a half points lower, and clear of zero too.
    draw study_1 ci_1 study_label_1 est_label_1

Blood pressure and blood sugar: close to zero, with ranges that run past it on both sides.
    draw study_2 ci_2 study_label_2 est_label_2 study_3 ci_3 study_label_3 est_label_3

And here economists read the same row differently.
    highlight study_3 ci_3 study_label_3 est_label_3 effect box

Some take it as evidence that coverage did not lower blood sugar within two years.
    highlight study_3 effect circle

Others note that only about one person in twenty had high blood sugar at all, so the range is too wide to rule out a worthwhile effect.
    highlight ci_3

And they point to what did move: new diabetes diagnoses and diabetes medicine rose clearly among the covered.
    highlight study_label_3 effect box

What would tell the two readings apart is many more people with diabetes, followed for longer, so that the range of chance shrinks.
    highlight ci_3

    quiz "A hospital wants to know whether a follow-up phone call after discharge keeps patients from coming back. Which comparison answers it?" intro "One new case."
        right: Picked at random: then nothing but the call separates the two groups, so each can stand in for the other's missing future.
        wrong: Who answers a hospital's call? Ask what else, besides the call, differs between the two groups.
        + Picked at random to be called, against not picked
        * Answered the call, against didn't answer
        * This year's patients, all called, against last year's

So what were we comparing when we said insurance improved her health? Maria with coverage, against Maria without.

One of those futures is always missing, for everyone, and no amount of data brings it back.

What we can do is borrow it, and a lottery makes the borrowing fair, on average and within a margin of chance.

Whether coverage helped Maria herself, we will never know. Whether it helps people like her, who sign up when coverage is offered, Oregon's draw began to answer.
    draw m_icon m_y1 m_q

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 500 y 540 font_size 26 style.opacity 0.6
